-- title:
--   The multiplication procedure of the HvdH machine is correct with cost G
-- statement:
--   Assume `StepOK st n₀ lin calls`, and let `iprog` be a five-procedure item-language program whose procedure 1 is the compiled multiplication routine `mulRM n₀ st` and whose procedures 2, 3, 4 are the DIF, DIT and bit-length procedures. Let $G:\mathbb N\to\mathbb N$ be monotone with, for every $N$,
--   $$\mathrm{topCost}(n_0,N) + \begin{cases}\mathrm{smallCost}(N) & N<n_0\\ \mathrm{lin}(N)+\sum_{(c,m)\in\mathrm{calls}(N)} c\,G(m) & N\ge n_0\end{cases} + 2 \le G(N).$$
--   Then for every bound $n$, procedure 1 correctly multiplies (`MulOK`) according to the spec `Mlt G n`: for all integers $a,b$ of bit length $<n$ and any state whose argument stack starts with $a,b$, calling procedure 1 replaces them by $a b$ within $G(\max(\mathrm{zsz}\,a,\mathrm{zsz}\,b))$ steps.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Proved by strong induction on $n$ (the recursive calls are on strictly smaller sizes). `IRuns I iprog p s s' c` is the big-step semantics of the item language with cost bound $c$. The machine layer: `IState HS` is a state with $64$ stacks of items (`Item` = integer or mark), an input position and an output; `RM` programs (straight-line register code `RS`, loops `rep`, conditionals, calls of the multiplication procedure through a `MulSpec` $M$ giving its domain and cost) have a functional semantics `p.run s`, a definedness predicate `p.ok M s` and a cost `p.cost M s`; `Spec M p s s' c` abbreviates `p.ok M s ∧ p.run s = s' ∧ p.cost M s ≤ c`. `ints L` / `gints U` push an integer list / a list of pairs as items (head = top); `zsz z` is the bit length of $|z|$. `MulBnd M m Φ` says the multiplication procedure accepts all pairs of integers of bit length $\le m$ at cost $\le Φ$. Register names (`GU`, `CR`, `KB`, `XN`, …) are fixed stack indices.
-- source:
--   D. Harvey, J. van der Hoeven, Integer multiplication in time O(n log n), Ann. of Math. 193 (2021), 563–617 (Theorem 1.1: multitape Turing machine multiplication in O(n log n)). Lemma `NLogN.Top.mul_ok_all` of the Lean development NLogN/HTopInd.lean (lines 28–53), written for the prove2.me integer-multiplication campaign.

